```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: pc1
diagnosis_text:
The fault is a misconfigured default gateway on pc1. The host's
routing table contains "default via 195.11.14.254 dev eth0", but
195.11.14.254 is a non-existent address — router1's actual eth1
address is 195.11.14.1/24. When pc1 attempts to send any packet
destined outside its own 195.11.14.0/24 subnet, the kernel selects
the default route and issues an ARP request for 195.11.14.254. That
ARP gets no reply (confirmed by the FAILED neighbor entry in pc1's
ARP table), so the kernel immediately returns "Destination Host
Unreachable" to the application, explaining the exact ICMP error the
user sees. pc1 can still ping 195.11.14.1 (router1) because that
address lies inside the directly-connected /24 prefix and its ARP
resolved successfully (STALE entry present). The reverse direction
also fails: packets from pc2 do reach pc1 via the routers (BGP is
healthy — router1 and router2 both have correct FIB entries for each
other's LAN), but pc1 cannot route its ICMP reply back to 200.1.1.2
because doing so again requires the (broken) default gateway, so pc2
sees only timeouts. Root cause: during the "network settings cleanup",
pc1's default route was set to 195.11.14.254 instead of 195.11.14.1.
Fix: on pc1 run `ip route replace default via 195.11.14.1 dev eth0`
(and update /etc/network/interfaces or the equivalent persistent
config to prevent recurrence on reboot).
=== END ===
```